Depression (Palliative Phase)
Palliative & End of Life
Pervasive sad mood, Complete anhedonia, Persistent hopelessness/despair

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-020
Reason for Visit: Acute crisis of severe despair, complete refusal of all care, and explicitly expressed suicidal ideation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has experienced a severe, rapid exacerbation of profound hopelessness and complete despair directly regarding the end-of-life process. The patient explicitly expressed active suicidal ideation and a profound, persistent desire for a hastened death tonight, absolutely refusing all routine medical and physical care. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was mandatory to conduct an acute psychiatric risk assessment, differentiate severe major depressive disorder from appropriate preparatory grief, and ensure immediate physical safety prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is absolutely no point to any of this anymore. I am just a burden, I want it to be over right now, and I won't take another pill."
History of Present Illness: Over the last month, the patient has exhibited a profound, unremitting explicitly depressed mood and severe pervasive sadness. There is complete anhedonia and a distinct, absolute loss of interest in all previously enjoyed activities, including visits from grandchildren. The patient expresses severe feelings of worthlessness, profound guilt, and feeling like an active, unmanageable burden to their spouse. Tonight, this escalated into explicit, profound hopelessness and active, distinctly expressed suicidal ideation with a persistent desire for hastened death. The spouse notes severe social withdrawal and active, persistent isolation from close family and friends over the last two weeks. During interactions, the patient displays a distinctly altered, profoundly flat emotional affect, followed by highly tearful spells. There is out of proportion cognitive slowing and an explicitly decreased ability to concentrate. Physical symptoms completely overlapping with the advanced disease include severe anorexia, fatigue, and intractable insomnia.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with assistance, but refusing to leave the bed due to severe apathy and despair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of self-harm or intentional medication overdose; severe caregiver emotional distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 85 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient is lying flat, turned away from the door, avoiding eye contact, with a profoundly flat and tearful affect.
Physical Exam: Physical examination is unremarkable for any acute physiological deterioration or new focal neurological deficits. The patient answers questions with extreme latency and monosyllabic responses. A structured clinical interview explicitly differentiates this state from appropriate preparatory grief, highlighting the severe, clinically paralyzing nature of the major depressive episode. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed pervasive sad mood (tearful, flat affect), complete anhedonia (refusing family visits), and explicitly voiced persistent hopelessness/despair ("no point to any of this").
Environmental Observations: The bedside table contains multiple bottles of prescribed opioids and sedatives, posing an immediate, highly accessible lethal means for overdose.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is currently refusing all evening medications, including baseline analgesia. Not currently prescribed any antidepressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Depression (Palliative Phase) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Suicidal Crisis)
Differentials:

Hypoactive Delirium - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient remaining fully oriented to time, place, and person, with intact (though slow) logical reasoning, contrasting with the severe inattention of delirium.

Appropriate Preparatory Grief - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of active suicidal ideation, profound feelings of worthlessness/guilt, and complete anhedonia, which exceed normal grief responses.

Uncontrolled physical pain - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient explicitly denies severe physical pain, citing pure emotional agony and despair as the reason for wanting to hasten death.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing an acute, severe major depressive crisis manifesting as profound despair and active suicidal ideation. While sadness is common in palliative care, the complete anhedonia, severe feelings of being a burden, and explicit desire for hastened death constitute a psychiatric emergency. Delaying assessment until normal hours would leave a severely vulnerable patient with direct access to lethal medications and a highly distressed spouse unable to manage the risk. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to perform a risk assessment, safely secure all medications, provide acute psychological first aid, and arrange urgent psychiatric and social work follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted an urgent suicide risk assessment. Instructed the spouse to immediately remove all medications from the bedroom and securely lock them away. Administered stat sublingual lorazepam to relieve the acute, overwhelming psychic tension and assist with the intractable insomnia tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the overwhelming wave of despair and the thought of wanting it to end immediately (trigger), you must explicitly promise to tell your spouse out loud (behavior). This allows your spouse to sit with you and share the burden, breaking the isolation of the dark thought and keeping you safe in that moment (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent, high-priority referral to the palliative care clinical psychologist and social worker for assessment first thing in the morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Immediate removal of all sharp objects and unsecured medications from the patient's vicinity.
Follow-up: Mandatory multidisciplinary palliative team review tomorrow; GP review to commence rapid-acting antidepressants (e.g., mirtazapine or methylphenidate depending on prognosis) in 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient makes an active attempt at self-harm, attempts to abscond from the house, or if the spouse feels entirely incapable of keeping the patient safe overnight, the spouse must call 000 immediately for police and ambulance attendance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe depression is a treatable complication of the illness, separate from the physical decline; it is not a personal failure or a requirement of the dying process.
Clinical Course: With psychological support and appropriate medication adjustments, the severe despair can be lifted, restoring a sense of dignity and connection in the remaining time.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Do not leave the patient alone under any circumstances tonight. Administer all medications yourself and keep the bottles secured.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Patient) The overwhelming darkness you are feeling is a severe chemical depression caused by the extreme stress of this situation. It is blinding you to the value you still hold to your family. The medication tonight will help you sleep, and tomorrow we will bring in expert support to help lift this heavy, unbearable weight.
Actionable Steps: Agree to a strict safety contract for tonight only: promise not to act on the thoughts until we can bring the specialist team tomorrow morning. Allow your spouse to manage all the pills.

